      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+4y=0,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+4{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =-2$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA=1.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=3.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2A{e}^{-2x}+-2Bx{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-2A+B=3.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $A=1$ gives $B=5.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-2x}+5x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$",
