    "q-130533": {
      "dom_id": "step-q130533",
      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+2y=0,\\,y(0)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-4.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+2{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1)-1+2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda -1 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =1\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=-2.\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\ny={e}^{x}(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=4.$ We differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{e}^{x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=-4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-2+Q=-4\\,⟹\\,Q=-2.\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-2$ and $Q=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
